  <h1>Is Trulicity Insulin? What Dulaglutide Actually Is (and Isn't)</h1>
  <div class="article-meta"><span>Updated July 2026</span><span>·</span><span>7 min read</span></div>

  <p>It comes in a pen, you inject it, and it treats diabetes — so plenty of people assume Trulicity is insulin. It isn't. Here's what dulaglutide actually does, and where it fits in the GLP-1 family.</p>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>

  <p>Trulicity is the brand name for <strong>dulaglutide</strong>, made by Eli Lilly — the same company behind Mounjaro. It belongs to the GLP-1 receptor agonist class, not the insulin family. The confusion is understandable: both come in injection pens, both treat type 2 diabetes, and both lower blood sugar. But they do it in completely different ways.</p>

  <h2 id="difference">Insulin vs a GLP-1: the actual difference</h2>

  <p><strong>Insulin</strong> is a replacement hormone. You inject it, it enters your bloodstream, and it directly pushes glucose out of your blood and into your cells. Dose it wrong and your blood sugar can drop dangerously low — hypoglycaemia is a constant consideration.</p>

  <p><strong>Dulaglutide</strong> works upstream. It mimics GLP-1, a gut hormone your body already makes, and essentially nudges your own pancreas to release insulin — but only in response to elevated blood sugar. It's glucose-dependent, which is why a GLP-1 on its own very rarely causes hypoglycaemia. It also slows stomach emptying and dials down appetite, the effects the whole class is now famous for.</p>

  <p>One drug replaces a hormone; the other coaches your body to use its own better. That's the whole distinction.</p>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <div class="callout__label">Where the Mix-Up Deepens</div>
    <p>Trulicity is often prescribed <em>in combination with</em> insulin when type 2 diabetes needs more than one line of treatment. Seeing both pens in the same fridge, it's easy to file them as the same kind of thing. They're teammates, not twins.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="used-for">What Trulicity is used for</h2>

  <p>Dulaglutide is licensed in the UK for <strong>type 2 diabetes</strong> — to improve blood sugar control alongside diet and exercise, either on its own (when metformin isn't suitable) or added to other diabetes medications. Its trials also showed cardiovascular benefit, reducing the risk of major events like heart attack and stroke in people with type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular risk factors, which is part of why it became so widely prescribed.</p>

  <p>It's taken <strong>once a week</strong>, from a single-use auto-injector pen that hides the needle — a design many needle-phobic users genuinely rate. Doses range from 0.75mg up to 4.5mg weekly.</p>

  <h3>What it's not used for</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Weight loss.</strong> Unlike semaglutide (Wegovy) and tirzepatide (Mounjaro), dulaglutide has no weight-management licence anywhere. People on it for diabetes do tend to lose some weight — typically a few kilograms — but it's a side benefit, not the job, and it's clearly the weakest of the modern GLP-1s on this front.</li>
    <li><strong>Type 1 diabetes.</strong> Type 1 requires insulin, full stop; a GLP-1 can't replace it.</li>
    <li><strong>Prediabetes.</strong> A question people search for, but it's not licensed for this. Prediabetes management runs through lifestyle programmes (and occasionally metformin), not Trulicity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="comparison">How it compares to the GLP-1s you hear more about</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Maker:</strong> Trulicity and Mounjaro are both Eli Lilly; Ozempic is Novo Nordisk.</li>
    <li><strong>Frequency:</strong> All three are once-weekly injections.</li>
    <li><strong>Licensed for weight loss?</strong> Trulicity: no. Ozempic: no (Wegovy is the weight-loss brand of semaglutide). Mounjaro: yes, in the UK.</li>
    <li><strong>Typical weight effect:</strong> Modest on Trulicity (a few kg); substantial on semaglutide at higher doses; largest of the three on tirzepatide.</li>
    <li><strong>Pen design:</strong> Trulicity is a single-use pen with a hidden needle and no dose dialling; Ozempic uses a multi-dose dial pen; Mounjaro comes as a KwikPen (multi-dose) in the UK.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="side-effects">Side effects</h2>

  <p>Classic GLP-1 territory: nausea, diarrhoea or constipation, reduced appetite, and occasional injection-site reactions, mostly during the first weeks or after a dose increase. Because dulaglutide's appetite effect is gentler than the newer drugs, many long-term users describe it as the easiest GLP-1 they've been on. The class-wide cautions — pancreatitis, gallbladder problems, and avoiding it with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid cancer — apply here too. Our <a href="/managing-glp1-nausea">nausea guide</a> covers the practical side.</p>

  <h2 id="bottom-line">The bottom line</h2>

  <p>Trulicity is a weekly GLP-1 injection for type 2 diabetes — a cousin of Ozempic and Mounjaro, not a form of insulin. If you've been prescribed it, you're on an appetite-and-blood-sugar drug with a long track record and a famously friendly pen. And if you arrived here hoping it was a weight-loss option: it isn't licensed for that, and its stablemate Mounjaro is the drug Lilly built for the job. For the full pharmacology, see our <a href="/dulaglutide">dulaglutide reference page</a>.</p>

